import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.D66Xq0Kv.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Backups and recovery","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/backups.md","filePath":"guide/backups.md"}'),r={name:"guide/backups.md"};function n(i,e,h,d,c,l){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="backups-and-recovery" tabindex="-1">Backups and recovery <a class="header-anchor" href="#backups-and-recovery" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Backups and recovery&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="storage" tabindex="-1">Storage <a class="header-anchor" href="#storage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Storage {#storage}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Add an S3-compatible storage under <strong>Backups</strong> (Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, AWS S3, MinIO or any other) with an access key limited to one bucket. Orbit writes, reads and deletes a probe object before accepting it.</p><p><strong>A Space on another server</strong> works too, without an account anywhere else. Choose it under <strong>Backups → Add storage</strong>, or take the suggestion the backups page makes when one of your Spaces runs on a different server than the data. Orbit creates a bucket called <code>orbit-backups</code> inside that Space, with an access key that reaches only that bucket, and checks it like any storage. Backups travel over the cluster network between your servers. The Space&#39;s own files and its own backups never include them.</p><ul><li>A database on the Space&#39;s own server cannot use it: both would go down with that server, so Orbit refuses it and the backups page leaves it unchecked.</li><li>The Space cannot be removed while it keeps backups; point those databases at another storage first.</li><li>Losing the Space&#39;s server loses the copies kept there. For data you cannot lose, keep an S3-compatible service too.</li><li>PostgreSQL&#39;s <a href="#point-in-time">point-in-time archive</a> still needs an S3-compatible service. A database whose backups go to a Space restores to the moment of each backup.</li></ul><h2 id="policy" tabindex="-1">The policy <a class="header-anchor" href="#policy" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The policy {#policy}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each database has its own: when it runs, in which time zone, how long backups are kept or how many copies to keep and where they go. Keeping backups on the server itself protects against mistakes, not against losing the server.</p><ul><li><strong>When:</strong> every hour or every few hours at a chosen minute, every day at a chosen time, on chosen weekdays, or a custom cron (<code>30 2 * * 1-5</code>). A database is backed up at most once an hour.</li><li><strong>Time zone:</strong> any IANA zone (<code>America/Sao_Paulo</code>, <code>Europe/Berlin</code>). Daylight saving changes are followed: a time that a change skips runs when the clock jumps.</li><li><strong>Retention:</strong> keep backups for 1–365 days, or keep the last 1–365 good copies. The default remains seven days. Seven days of hourly backups means about 168 copies; seven daily copies means about seven days. Manual and scheduled backups follow the same policy, so manual backups shorten the time covered by a count.</li><li><strong>Cleanup:</strong> after every backup that was stored, whatever its restore test said, and once a day for every database with backups turned on. Count retention keeps the newest verified copies and copies whose restore test passed, was skipped or ran out of time; failed attempts do not displace them. It then removes extra copies and failed attempts from Orbit and storage. Pending backups and the newest good copy are always protected. Deleting a database deletes its copies from storage too. With bucket versioning, deletes may leave older object versions: configure the provider’s lifecycle rules to remove those versions too.</li><li><strong>Restore tests:</strong> after every backup, on the first backup of each day, on the first of each week, or never. See <a href="#restore-tests">Restore tests</a>.</li><li>The policy shows the next three backups before you save it.</li></ul><h2 id="safety" tabindex="-1">Is it safe? <a class="header-anchor" href="#safety" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Is it safe? {#safety}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every database and Space card answers three questions about its backups:</p><ul><li><strong>On a schedule:</strong> backups are on and have a schedule.</li><li><strong>Off this server:</strong> they go to an S3 storage that is ready, so a copy survives losing the server the data lives on. Backups kept on the same server do not.</li><li><strong>Restore tested:</strong> a restore test passed in the last 14 days.</li></ul><p>Each answer is green or amber, and the first amber one comes with what to do next. Outside production, backups that are off read as a choice: the card says so instead of showing three amber answers. When databases or Spaces keep their only backup on the server they run on, the overview says how many and on which server, with a link to the backups.</p><p><strong>Keep a copy off the server</strong>, on the Backups page, fixes them together. Choose a ready storage, keep the databases and Spaces you want selected, and <strong>Send</strong> changes each one&#39;s destination to that storage, one at a time, and takes a backup there right away unless you clear the option. Each line says when its backups go to the storage, or why the change failed. Backups already taken stay where they are until retention removes them. Without a ready storage, the panel asks you to add one first: any S3-compatible service outside your servers works, such as Cloudflare R2, which has a free tier, Backblaze B2 or AWS S3.</p><p>A new database starts with nightly backups. When the workspace has a ready S3 storage, they go there from the first one; otherwise the dialog says they stay on the server until you add one. Every backup gets a restore test unless you choose a different cadence in <a href="#restore-tests">restore tests</a>.</p><h2 id="verified" tabindex="-1">Verified backups <a class="header-anchor" href="#verified" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verified backups {#verified}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A backup only shows as <strong>verified</strong> after Orbit downloaded it again and restored it in an isolated container that nothing can reach. A backup that fails that test stays stored but unverified, and the operation shows why. A backup that was not tested shows as <strong>Not restore-tested</strong>.</p><p>A backup is a <code>pg_dump</code> in custom format for PostgreSQL, a <code>mysqldump</code> file for MySQL, a <code>mongodump --archive --gzip</code> file for MongoDB, an RDB file for Redis or a tar of the objects for a <a href="./spaces">Space</a>. It is uploaded with its SHA-256 and read back before it counts as stored.</p><p>For PostgreSQL and MySQL, <code>pg_dump</code> and <code>mysqldump</code> run in a short-lived container next to the database, on the same server and with a memory limit of its own, so the dump normally has a separate memory budget. If Orbit cannot start that container, it falls back to the database container and records the reason as a warning in the operation log. MongoDB and Spaces run their tools in the database&#39;s <code>client</code> container for the same reason.</p><h2 id="restarting" tabindex="-1">When the database is restarting <a class="header-anchor" href="#restarting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When the database is restarting {#restarting}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Before taking a snapshot, Orbit waits up to three minutes for the database pod to be ready. For CloudNativePG, the cluster must also report a healthy phase and its primary must be ready. PostgreSQL shutdown, startup and recovery errors, refused connections and temporary MySQL disconnects trigger up to three retries, spaced across a five-minute retry window. Each retry discards any partial dump; imports are not retried this way.</p><p>If the database stays unavailable, the operation reports <code>backupDatabaseRestarting</code> with the pod’s last termination, exit code and restart count, recent Kubernetes events and the CloudNativePG phase and reason. An <code>OOMKilled</code> termination also offers the database’s memory settings and any open memory incident. Fix the reported cause, wait for the database to become ready, then retry the backup.</p><h2 id="restore-tests" tabindex="-1">Restore tests <a class="header-anchor" href="#restore-tests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Restore tests {#restore-tests}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A restore test copies the backup into a fresh, isolated database, restores it and counts its tables and rows, keys for Redis or objects for a Space. Its log shows how long each part took: starting the database, copying the backup in, restoring and counting.</p><ul><li><strong>How often:</strong> by default, after every backup when the database is backed up at most once a day, and on the first backup of each day when it is backed up more often. The policy can choose after every backup, daily, weekly or never. Days and weeks follow the policy&#39;s time zone.</li><li><strong>After a failure:</strong> when the last test did not pass, the next backup is tested too, whatever the policy says, until one passes. A test that ran out of time is not a failure: the next test follows the policy.</li><li><strong>By hand:</strong> backups taken with <strong>Back up now</strong> are tested unless the policy says never, and <strong>Test now</strong> tests any stored backup. A test waits for a backup or another test of the same database to finish, so they never run at the same time.</li><li><strong>Time limit:</strong> 10 minutes, plus 5 more for every 100 MB of the database&#39;s data on disk, or of the backup when that is larger. A test that reaches it is stopped and reported, and the backup stays restorable as <strong>Not tested in time</strong>: its checksum matched, only the test did not finish. The backup&#39;s operation gets the same time on top of its own 30 minutes, so a large database is not cut short. The throwaway PostgreSQL uses the database&#39;s memory for the restore and runs it with up to four jobs.</li><li><strong>Where the time goes:</strong> while a test runs, the operation card shows the latest line of the step in progress and how long that step has taken: starting the isolated database, copying the backup into it, the restore itself and the row count.</li><li><strong>Data at risk</strong> counts from the newest backup you can restore, tested or not. <strong>Time to restore</strong> says when the restore test it comes from ran.</li></ul><h2 id="restoring" tabindex="-1">Restoring <a class="header-anchor" href="#restoring" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Restoring {#restoring}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Choose a backup on the database&#39;s page and type the database&#39;s name. A verified backup can be restored, and so can one that was not tested yet, since its SHA-256 matched; one whose restore test did not pass cannot.</p><ol><li>By default Orbit first backs up the current data, so there is always a way back.</li><li>A PostgreSQL restore runs in one transaction, so it either completes or leaves the data untouched.</li><li>The applications that use the database are restarted, and Orbit compares the restored tables and rows (or keys) with what the backup&#39;s test found, when it was tested.</li></ol><h2 id="importing" tabindex="-1">Importing data <a class="header-anchor" href="#importing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Importing data {#importing}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Import data</strong> brings data from elsewhere as a backup marked <em>Imported</em>. It goes through the same checksum and isolated test, and nothing changes until you restore it.</p><ul><li><strong>From another database:</strong> paste its connection URL with the password (<code>postgresql://</code>, <code>mysql://</code>, <code>mongodb://</code>, <code>mongodb+srv://</code> or <code>redis://</code>). A MongoDB URL names the database to bring, as in <code>mongodb+srv://user:password@cluster0.example.net/shop</code>. The dump runs on your server, so the source must accept connections from the server&#39;s IP. The URL is sealed while the import runs, erased when it ends and never written to the logs.</li><li><strong>From a file:</strong> a custom-format <code>pg_dump -Fc</code> file, a <code>mysqldump</code> <code>.sql</code> file, a <code>mongodump --archive --gzip</code> file of one database, a <code>dump.rdb</code> or, for a Space, a tar of the files made inside their folder, up to 64 GiB.</li><li>PostgreSQL&#39;s <code>pg_dump</code> refuses a server newer than itself, so import from the same major version or an older one.</li></ul><h2 id="point-in-time" tabindex="-1">Point-in-time recovery <a class="header-anchor" href="#point-in-time" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Point-in-time recovery {#point-in-time}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A PostgreSQL database on CloudNativePG whose policy sends backups to external storage also archives its WAL there, continuously.</p><ul><li><strong>Turning it on:</strong> saving such a policy restarts the database once and starts a first base backup. Every later backup also takes a base backup.</li><li><strong>The archive:</strong> in the same bucket, under <code>&lt;prefix&gt;/orbit/pitr/&lt;database id&gt;</code>, with a time window derived from the policy. Days mode uses the chosen days. Count mode multiplies the number of copies by the longest interval between the next 40 scheduled runs, rounded up to whole days with a minimum of one. Seven daily copies therefore keep a seven-day archive; seven hourly copies keep at least one day. A policy without a usable schedule uses one day per copy. A WAL file that is not full is archived after a minute at most. After a major upgrade it starts over from a new base backup.</li><li><strong>The window:</strong> the database&#39;s page shows it, from the oldest base backup kept to now. When WAL files wait to be archived, or archiving fails, it ends at the last file archived. A failing archive, or WAL waiting for more than 10 minutes, marks the database as degraded.</li></ul><p><strong>Restore to a point in time</strong> takes any second in the window and the database&#39;s name:</p><ol><li>Orbit keeps a copy of the current data, like any restore.</li><li>It recovers the archive into a temporary cluster at that moment and copies its data into the database in one transaction.</li><li>It restarts the applications that use the database and checks the tables and rows.</li></ol><p>On a test cluster, restoring to the second before a destructive change took about a minute.</p><h2 id="rebuild" tabindex="-1">Rebuilding from the archive <a class="header-anchor" href="#rebuild" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rebuilding from the archive {#rebuild}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When no instance of the database answers (every server or volume holding it is gone), the page says so and offers <strong>Rebuild from the archive</strong>, with the database&#39;s name to confirm.</p><ol><li>Orbit removes what is left of the instances and their volumes.</li><li>It recovers the last base backup and every archived WAL file into new instances on the same servers. They archive under a new name in the same folder, so the WAL they came from stays untouched.</li><li>It restarts the applications that use the database, checks the credentials and takes a first base backup, which opens the window again.</li></ol><p>Writes that had not reached the archive are lost: up to about a minute of them. The activity says up to which commit the data came back. Orbit refuses the rebuild while the database still has a primary that answers.</p><h2 id="orbit" tabindex="-1">Backing up Orbit itself <a class="header-anchor" href="#orbit" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Backing up Orbit itself {#orbit}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Database backups do not include Orbit&#39;s own data. See <a href="./operating#backup">Running Orbit</a> for <code>orbit backup</code> and <code>orbit restore</code>.</p>',42)])])}const b=t(r,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,b as default};
